(p/'DERIVATION.md').write_text('# Universal ideal identities before class relations\n\nFor arbitrary z,w in I_p, write re(z)=p*a and re(w)=p*c. Write210=p*q. Their product is p times the actual integral quadratic element with coordinates p*a*c-q*im(z)*im(w) and a*im(w)+im(z)*c. Hence I_p*I_p lies in the principal ideal(p), by native ideal-product generation.\n\nBoth p*p and theta*theta=-210 belong to I_p*I_p. An explicit identity A*p^2-C*210=p therefore puts p itself in I_p*I_p. Supply the four exact pairs (53,1),(47,2),(17,2),(13,3) for primes2,3,5,7. This proves both inclusions of the literal ideals universally.\n\nNative Ideal.map along integerEquiv identifies the old kernel with the exact O_K kernel of the native reduction map. Surjectivity of integerEquiv proves the reverse inclusion, so no equality of cardinalities substitutes for ideal equality. The native map preserves products and maps the principal ideal(p) to the same principal ideal(p) in O_K. This yields the actual native principal-square theorem.\n\nThe preceding residue packet proves each ideal nonzero. Package it in the native nonzero-ideal monoid and apply the actual ClassGroup.mk0 homomorphism. Its power law and native mk0_eq_one_iff turn the produced principal square into class^2=1. The inverse identity follows in the native group. This is genuinely about the original ideal classes, but it does not prove that any class is nontrivial or that the four classes generate the full class group.\n')
(p/'regress_cm_prime_squares_rebuild.py').write_text("from pathlib import Path\nfrom itertools import product\nimport json\ncount=0\nfor p,A,C in [(2,53,1),(3,47,2),(5,17,2),(7,13,3)]:\n assert A*p*p-C*210==p\n q=210//p\n for a,b,c,d in product(range(-5,6),repeat=4):\n  left=(p*p*a*c-210*b*d,p*a*d+p*b*c)\n  witness=(p*a*c-q*b*d,a*d+b*c)\n  assert left==(p*witness[0],p*witness[1]);count+=1\n# wrong sign and missing Bezout coefficient fail\nassert 53*4+210!=2 and 47*9-210!=3\nout={'status':'PASS','universal_product_identity_tests':count,'exact_bezout_identities':4,'negative_controls':2,'scope':'Exact regression only; no Lean or class-number assertion.'}\nPath(__file__).with_name('REGRESSION.json').write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2)+'\\n');print(out)\n")
